@node	1.3, Chapter 2, 1.2, Chapter 1
@section Formulating Abstractions with Higher-Order Procedures

We have seen that procedures are, in effect, abstractions that describe
compound operations on numbers independent of the particular numbers.  For
example, when we

@example kotlin
public fun cube(x: Long): Long = x * x * x
@end example

@noindent
we are not talking about the cube of a particular number, but rather about a
method for obtaining the cube of any number.  Of course we could get along
without ever defining this procedure, by always writing expressions such as

@example kotlin
3L * 3L * 3L
x * x * x
y * y * y
@end example

@noindent
and never mentioning @code{cube} explicitly.  This would place us at a serious
disadvantage, forcing us to work always at the level of the particular
operations that happen to be primitives in the language (multiplication, in
this case) rather than in terms of higher-level operations.  Our programs would
be able to compute cubes, but our language would lack the ability to express
the concept of cubing.  One of the things we should demand from a powerful
programming language is the ability to build abstractions by assigning names to
common patterns and then to work in terms of the abstractions directly.
Procedures provide this ability.  This is why all but the most primitive
programming languages include mechanisms for defining procedures.

Yet even in numerical processing we will be severely limited in our ability to
create abstractions if we are restricted to procedures whose parameters must be
numbers.  Often the same programming pattern will be used with a number of
different procedures.  To express such patterns as concepts, we will need to
construct procedures that can accept procedures as arguments or return
procedures as values.  Procedures that manipulate procedures are called
@newterm{higher-order procedures}.  This section shows how higher-order
procedures can serve as powerful abstraction mechanisms, vastly increasing the
expressive power of our language.

@menu
* 1.3.1::            Procedures as Arguments
* 1.3.2::            Constructing Procedures Using @code{Lambda}
* 1.3.3::            Procedures as General Methods
* 1.3.4::            Procedures as Returned Values
@end menu

@node	1.3.1, 1.3.2, 1.3, 1.3
@subsection Procedures as Arguments

Consider the following three procedures.  The first computes the sum of the
integers from @code{a} through @code{b}:

@example kotlin
fun sumIntegers(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = if (a > b) 0L else a + sumIntegers(a + 1L, b)
@end example

@noindent
The second computes the sum of the cubes of the integers in the given range:

@example kotlin
fun sumCubes(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = if (a > b) 0L else cube(a) + sumCubes(a + 1L, b)
@end example

@noindent
The third computes the sum of a sequence of terms in the series
@displaymath
{1 \over 1\cdot 3} +  {1 \over 5\cdot 7} + {1 \over 9\cdot 11} + {\dots,}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
  1       1       1
----- + ----- + ------ + ...
1 * 3   5 * 7   9 * 11
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which converges to @math{{\pi / 8}} (very slowly):@footnote{This series, usually
written in the equivalent form 
@math{{\pi\over4} = {1 - {1\over3} + {1\over5}} - {{1\over7} + \dots}}, 
is due to Leibniz.  We'll see how to use this as the basis for some
fancy numerical tricks in @ref{3.5.3}.}

@example kotlin
fun piSum(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Double = if (a > b) 0.0 else 1.0 / (a * (a + 2L)) + piSum(a + 4L, b)
@end example

@noindent
These three procedures clearly share a common underlying pattern.  They are for
the most part identical, differing only in the name of the procedure, the
function of @code{a} used to compute the term to be added, and the function
that provides the next value of @code{a}.  We could generate each of the
procedures by filling in slots in the same template:

@example kotlin
fun ⟨name⟩(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = if (a > b) 0L else ⟨term⟩(a) + ⟨name⟩(⟨next⟩(a), b)
@end example

@noindent
The presence of such a common pattern is strong evidence that there is a useful
abstraction waiting to be brought to the surface.  Indeed, mathematicians long
ago identified the abstraction of @newterm{summation of a series} and invented
``sigma notation,'' for example
@displaymath
{\sum_{n = a}^b f(n)} \,=\, {f(a)} + \dots + {f(b),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
  b
 ---
 >    f(n) = f(a) + ... + f(b)
 ---
 n=a
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
to express this concept.  The power of sigma notation is that it allows
mathematicians to deal with the concept of summation itself rather than only
with particular sums---for example, to formulate general results about sums
that are independent of the particular series being summed.

Similarly, as program designers, we would like our language to be powerful
enough so that we can write a procedure that expresses the concept of summation
itself rather than only procedures that compute particular sums.  We can do so
readily in our procedural language by taking the common template shown above
and transforming the ``slots'' into formal parameters:

@example kotlin
fun sum(
    term: (Long) -> Long,
    a: Long,
    next: (Long) -> Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = if (a > b) 0L else term(a) + sum(term, next(a), next, b)
@end example

@noindent
Notice that @code{sum} takes as its arguments the lower and upper bounds
@code{a} and @code{b} together with the functions @code{term} and @code{next},
whose types, @code{(Long) -> Long}, are Kotlin's way of saying ``a function
from one @code{Long} to another.''  We can use @code{sum} just as we would any
function.  For example, we can use it (along with a function @code{inc} that
increments its argument by 1) to define @code{sumCubes}:

@example kotlin
fun inc(n: Long): Long = n + 1L

fun sumCubes(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = sum(::cube, a, ::inc, b)
@end example

@noindent
Using this, we can compute the sum of the cubes of the integers from 1 to 10:

@example kotlin
sumCubes(1L, 10L)
// => 3025
@end example

@noindent
With the aid of an identity function to compute the term, we can define
@code{sumIntegers} in terms of @code{sum}:

@example kotlin
fun identity(x: Long): Long = x

fun sumIntegers(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Long = sum(::identity, a, ::inc, b)
@end example

@noindent
Then we can add up the integers from 1 to 10:

@example kotlin
sumIntegers(1L, 10L)
// => 55
@end example

@noindent
We can also define @code{piSum} in the same way:@footnote{Notice that we have
used block structure (@ref{1.1.8}) to embed the definitions of
@code{piNext} and @code{piTerm} within @code{piSum}, since these functions
are unlikely to be useful for any other purpose.  We will see how to get rid of
them altogether in @ref{1.3.2}.}

@example kotlin
fun piSum(
    a: Long,
    b: Long,
): Double {
    fun piTerm(x: Long): Double = 1.0 / (x * (x + 2L))

    fun piNext(x: Long): Long = x + 4L
    return if (a > b) 0.0 else piTerm(a) + piSum(piNext(a), b)
}
@end example

@noindent
Using these functions, we can compute an approximation to @math{\pi}:

@example kotlin
8.0 * piSum(1L, 1000L)
// => 3.139592655589783
@end example

@noindent
Once we have @code{sum}, we can use it as a building block in formulating
further concepts.  For instance, the definite integral of a function @math{f}
between the limits @math{a} and @math{b} can be approximated numerically using the
formula
@displaymath
{\int_a^b \kern-0.3em f}
  \;=\; {\left[\;f\left(a + {d x \over 2}\right)\right.}
  \,+\, {f\left(a + dx + {dx \over 2}\right)}
  \,+\, {\left. f\left(a + 2dx + {dx \over 2}\right)
  \,+\, \dots \;\right] dx}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
/b     /  /     dx \    /          dx \    /           dx \      \
|  f = | f| a + -- | + f| a + dx + -- | + f| a + 2dx + -- | + ...| dx
/a     \  \     2  /    \          2  /    \           2  /      /
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
for small values of @math{{dx}}.  We can express this directly, this time
over @code{Double}, so @code{sum}'s real-valued overload applies:

@example kotlin
fun sum(
    term: (Double) -> Double,
    a: Double,
    next: (Double) -> Double,
    b: Double,
): Double = if (a > b) 0.0 else term(a) + sum(term, next(a), next, b)

fun cube(x: Double): Double = x * x * x

fun integral(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    a: Double,
    b: Double,
    dx: Double,
): Double {
    fun addDx(x: Double): Double = x + dx
    return sum(f, a + dx / 2.0, ::addDx, b) * dx
}

integral(::cube, 0.0, 1.0, 0.01)
// => 0.24998750000000042

integral(::cube, 0.0, 1.0, 0.001)
// => 0.249999875000001
@end example

@noindent
(The exact value of the integral of @code{cube} between 0 and 1 is 1/4.)  A
second @code{sum} may look surprising after chapter 1.1's promise that a name
means one thing; overload resolution is the compiler's job of picking
@emph{which} @code{sum} an argument list means, decided entirely from the
argument types, the same way it already decided between the @code{Long} and
@code{Double} @code{square} of section 1.1.4 and 1.1.7.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.29}Exercise 1.29:} Simpson's Rule is a more accurate
method of numerical integration than the method illustrated above.  Using
