@displaymath
{h \over 3}(y_0 + {4y_1} + {2y_2} + {4y_3} + {2y_4} + \dots + {2y_{n-2}} + {4y_{n-1} + y_n),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
h
- (y_0 + 4y_1 + 2y_2 + 4y_3 + 2y_4 + ... + 2y_(n-2) + 4y_(n-1) + y_n)
3
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
where @math{{h = (b - a)/n}}, for some even integer @math{n}, and
@math{y_k = {f(a + kh)}}.  (Increasing @math{n} increases the
accuracy of the approximation.)  Write a function that takes as arguments
@math{f}, @math{a}, @math{b}, and @math{n} and returns the value of the integral, computed
using Simpson's Rule.  Use your function to integrate @code{cube} between 0
and 1 (with @math{{n = 100}} and @math{{n = 1000}}), and compare the results to those of
the @code{integral} function shown above.
@end quotation

