@node	1.3.2, 1.3.3, 1.3.1, 1.3
@subsection Constructing Procedures Using @code{Lambda}

In using @code{sum} as in @ref{1.3.1}, it seems terribly awkward to
have to define trivial functions such as @code{piTerm} and @code{piNext}
just so we can use them as arguments to our higher-order function.  Rather
than define @code{piNext} and @code{piTerm}, it would be more convenient to
have a way to directly write ``the function that returns its input
incremented by 4'' and ``the function that returns the reciprocal of its input
times its input plus 2.''  Kotlin gives us this with the @newterm{lambda
expression}, written as arguments in curly braces.  We can write

@example kotlin
{ x: Double -> x + 4.0 }
@end example

@noindent
and

@example kotlin
{ x: Double -> 1.0 / (x * (x + 2.0)) }
@end example

@noindent
Then our @code{piSum} function can be written without defining any
auxiliary functions as

@example kotlin
fun piSumWithLambdas(
    a: Double,
    b: Double,
): Double = sum({ x -> 1.0 / (x * (x + 2.0)) }, a, { x -> x + 4.0 }, b)
@end example

@noindent
Again using lambda expressions, we can write the @code{integral} function
without having to define the auxiliary function @code{addDx}:

@example kotlin
fun integralWithLambda(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    a: Double,
    b: Double,
    dx: Double,
): Double = sum(f, a + dx / 2.0, { x -> x + dx }, b) * dx
@end example

@noindent
In general, a lambda expression is written the same way as a function's body,
except that no name is given to the function itself:

@example kotlin
{ ⟨formal-parameters⟩ -> ⟨body⟩ }
@end example

@noindent
The resulting function is just as much a function as one that is written with
@code{fun}.  The only difference is that it has not been associated with any
name in the environment.  In fact,

@example kotlin
fun plus4Fun(x: Long): Long = x + 4L
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example kotlin
val plus4Val: (Long) -> Long = { x -> x + 4L }
@end example

@noindent
We can read this lambda expression as follows:

@example
{     x        ->     x    +    4  }
      |               |         |
the function of an argument x that adds x and 4
@end example

@noindent
Like any expression that has a function as its value, a lambda expression can
be used as the operator in a call such as

@example kotlin
({ x: Long, y: Long, z: Long -> x + y + z * z })(1L, 2L, 3L)
// => 12
@end example

@noindent
or, more generally, in any context where we would normally use a function
name.@footnote{Lambda calculus, a mathematical formalism introduced by the
mathematical logician Alonzo @ref{Church (1941)}, gave the book's original
language its name for this construct.  Church developed the 
λ-calculus to provide a rigorous foundation for studying the 
notions of function
and function application.  The λ-calculus has become a basic tool
for mathematical investigations of the semantics of programming languages.
Kotlin's own name for the same construct, the lambda expression, keeps that
history visible even though the keyword @code{lambda} itself is gone.}

@subsubheading Using @code{let} to create local variables

Another use of a lambda expression is in creating local variables.  We often
need local variables in our functions other than those that have been bound
as formal parameters.  For example, suppose we wish to compute the function
@displaymath
{f(x,y)} \,=\, {x(1 + xy)^2} + {y(1 - y)} + {(1 + xy)(1 - y),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x,y) = x(1 + xy)^2 + y(1 - y) + (1 + xy)(1 - y)
@end example

@end ifinfo
which we could also express as
@displaymath
\eqalign{
  a                     &=   {1 + xy,}  \cr
  \hphantom{(x,y)} b    &=   {1 - y,}   \cr
  {f(x,y)}              &=   {xa^2} + {yb} + {ab.}
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
     a = 1 + xy
     b = 1 - y
f(x,y) = xa^2 + yb + ab
@end example

@end ifinfo
In writing a function to compute @math{f}, we would like to include as local
variables not only @math{x} and @math{y} but also the names of intermediate
quantities like @math{a} and @math{b}.  One way to accomplish this is to use an
auxiliary function to bind the local variables:

@example kotlin
fun fWithHelper(
    x: Double,
    y: Double,
): Double {
    fun fHelper(
        a: Double,
        b: Double,
    ): Double = x * a * a + y * b + a * b
    return fHelper(1.0 + x * y, 1.0 - y)
}
@end example

@noindent
This construct is so useful that Kotlin's ordinary local @code{val} plays
its role directly: naming @code{a} and @code{b} as local @code{val}s inside
@code{f}'s body:

@example kotlin
fun f(
    x: Double,
    y: Double,
): Double {
    val a = 1.0 + x * y
    val b = 1.0 - y
    return x * a * a + y * b + a * b
}
@end example

@noindent
No new mechanism is required to provide local variables; a @code{val} binding
is already exactly the mechanism the book's @code{let} introduces, so no
underlying lambda application needs to be shown separately, the way section
1.1.2 already established.

We can see from @code{val}'s own scoping rule that a name it binds is visible
from the point of its declaration to the end of the enclosing block.  This
implies that:

@itemize @bullet

@item
A @code{val} can be bound as locally as possible to where it is to be used.
For example, if the value of @code{x} is 5, the value of the expression

@example kotlin
run {
    val x = 3L
    x + x * 10L
} + x
@end example

@noindent
is 38.  Here, the @code{x} inside @code{run}'s block is 3 (a fresh, inner
binding shadowing the outer one for the block's own body), so the value of
the block is 33.  On the other hand, the @code{x} that is the second argument
to the outermost @code{+} is still the outer 5.

@item
A binding's initializer is evaluated before that binding comes into scope, so
an earlier name of the same spelling still means the outer binding while a
later @code{val}'s initializer runs.  This matters when the expression that
provides the value for a new local variable depends upon a variable having the
same name as the local variable itself.  For example, if the value of the
outer @code{x} is 2, the value of

@example kotlin
val y = x + 2L
run {
    val x = 3L
    x * y
}
@end example

@noindent
is 12, because @code{y} was computed from the @emph{outer} @code{x} (giving
4) before the inner @code{x} was ever declared, and the inner @code{x} is 3;
@code{3 * 4} is 12.  A single @code{let} block in the book's language binds
all of its names from initializers evaluated together, before any of the new
names are visible; reproducing that shape in Kotlin, where each @code{val}
binds as soon as it is declared, means naming the outer-scoped value first
under its own name, as shown.

@end itemize

@noindent
Sometimes we can use local @code{fun} definitions to get the same effect as
with local @code{val}s.  For example, we could have defined the function
@math{f} above as

@example kotlin
fun f(x: Double, y: Double): Double {
    fun a(): Double = 1.0 + x * y
    fun b(): Double = 1.0 - y
    return x * a() * a() + y * b() + a() * b()
}
@end example

@noindent
We prefer, however, to use @code{val} in situations like this and to reserve
local @code{fun} for auxiliary functions.@footnote{Understanding
local @code{val} and local @code{fun} well enough to be sure that a program
means what we intend requires a more elaborate model of the evaluation process
than we have presented in this chapter.  The subtleties do not arise with
local definitions of functions, however.  We will return to this issue in
@ref{4.1.6}, after we learn more about evaluation.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.34}Exercise 1.34:} Suppose we define the function

@example kotlin
fun f(g: (Long) -> Long): Long = g(2L)
@end example

@noindent
Then we have

@example kotlin
f(::square)
// => 4

f { z -> z * (z + 1L) }
// => 6
@end example

@noindent
What happens if we ask the compiler to check @code{f(::f)}?  Explain.
@end quotation

@node	1.3.3, 1.3.4, 1.3.2, 1.3
@subsection Procedures as General Methods

We introduced compound procedures in @ref{1.1.4} as a mechanism for
abstracting patterns of numerical operations so as to make them independent of
the particular numbers involved.  With higher-order procedures, such as the
@code{integral} function of @ref{1.3.1}, we began to see a more
powerful kind of abstraction: functions used to express general methods of
computation, independent of the particular functions involved.  In this section
we discuss two more elaborate examples---general methods for finding
zeros and fixed points of functions---and show how these methods can be expressed
directly as functions.

@subsubheading Finding roots of equations by the half-interval method

The @newterm{half-interval method} is a simple but powerful technique for
finding roots of an equation @math{{f(x) = 0}}, where @math{f} is a continuous
function.  The idea is that, if we are given points @math{a} and @math{b} such that
@math{{f(a) < 0 < f(b)}}, then @math{f} must have at least one zero between
@math{a} and @math{b}.  To locate a zero, let @math{x} be the average of @math{a}
and @math{b} and compute @math{{f(x)}}.  If @math{{f(x) > 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{a} and @math{x}.  If @math{{f(x) < 0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero
between @math{x} and @math{b}.  Continuing in this way, we can identify smaller and
smaller intervals on which @math{f} must have a zero.  When we reach a point
where the interval is small enough, the process stops.  Since the interval of
uncertainty is reduced by half at each step of the process, the number of steps
required grows as @math{{\Theta(\log(L /\, T))}}, where @math{L} is the
length of the original interval and @math{T} is the error tolerance (that is, the
size of the interval we will consider ``small enough'').  Here
is a function that implements this strategy:

@example kotlin
fun search(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    negPoint: Double,
    posPoint: Double,
): Double {
    val midpoint = average(negPoint, posPoint)
    if (closeEnough(negPoint, posPoint)) return midpoint
    val testValue = f(midpoint)
    return when {
        testValue > 0.0 -> search(f, negPoint, midpoint)
        testValue < 0.0 -> search(f, midpoint, posPoint)
        else -> midpoint
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
We assume that we are initially given the points at which the
function @math{f} together with its values are negative and positive.
We first compute the midpoint of the two given points.  Next we check
to see if the given interval is small enough, and if so we simply
return the midpoint as our answer.  Otherwise, we compute as a test
value the value of @math{f} at the midpoint.  If the test value is
positive, then we continue the process with a new interval running from
the original negative point to the midpoint.  If the test value is
negative, we continue with the interval from the midpoint to the positive
point.  Finally, there is the possibility that the test value is 0, in
which case the midpoint is itself the root we are searching for.  To test whether the
endpoints are ``close enough'' we can use a function similar to the one used
in @ref{1.1.7} for computing square roots:@footnote{We have used
0.001 as a representative ``small'' number to indicate a tolerance for the
acceptable error in a calculation.  The appropriate tolerance for a real
calculation depends upon the limitations of the computer and the nature of the
algorithm.  This is often a very subtle consideration, requiring help from a
numerical analyst or some other kind of magician.}

@example kotlin
fun closeEnough(
    x: Double,
    y: Double,
): Boolean = abs(x - y) < 0.001
@end example

@noindent
@code{Search} is awkward to use directly, because we can accidentally give it
points at which @math{f}'s values do not have the required sign, in which case we
get a wrong answer.  Instead we will use @code{search} via the following
function, which checks to see which of the endpoints has a negative function
value and which has a positive value, and calls the @code{search} function
accordingly.  If the function has the same sign on the two given points, the
half-interval method cannot be used, in which case the function signals an
error.@footnote{This can be accomplished by raising a domain error, the way
@ref{0.6} introduced.}

@example kotlin
public sealed interface HalfIntervalError {
    public data class SameSign(
        val a: Double,
        val b: Double,
    ) : HalfIntervalError
}

context(r: Raise<HalfIntervalError>)
fun halfIntervalMethod(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    a: Double,
    b: Double,
): Double {
    val aValue = f(a)
    val bValue = f(b)
    return when {
        aValue < 0.0 && bValue > 0.0 -> search(f, a, b)
        bValue < 0.0 && aValue > 0.0 -> search(f, b, a)
        else -> r.raise(HalfIntervalError.SameSign(a, b))
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The following example uses the half-interval method to approximate @math{\pi}
as the root between 2 and 4 of @math{{\sin x = 0}}:

@example kotlin
halfIntervalMethod(::sin, 2.0, 4.0)
// => 3.14111328125
@end example

@noindent
Here is another example, using the half-interval method to search for
a root of the equation @math{{x^3 - 2x - 3 = 0}} between 1 and 2:

@example kotlin
halfIntervalMethod({ x -> x * x * x - 2.0 * x - 3.0 }, 1.0, 2.0)
// => 1.89306640625
@end example

@subsubheading Finding fixed points of functions

A number @math{x} is called a @newterm{fixed point} of a function @math{f} if @math{x}
satisfies the equation @math{{f(x) = x}}.  For some functions @math{f} we can
locate a fixed point by beginning with an initial guess and applying @math{f}
repeatedly,
@displaymath
{f(x),}\quad {f(f(x)),}\quad {f(f(f(x))),}\quad \dots
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x), f(f(x)), f(f(f(x))), ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
until the value does not change very much.  Using this idea, we can devise a
function @code{fixedPoint} that takes as inputs a function and an initial
guess and produces an approximation to a fixed point of the function.  We apply
the function repeatedly until we find two successive values whose difference
is less than some prescribed tolerance:

@example kotlin
private const val TOLERANCE = 0.00001

fun fixedPoint(
    f: (Double) -> Double,
    firstGuess: Double,
): Double {
    fun closeEnough(guess: Double, next: Double): Boolean = abs(guess - next) < TOLERANCE
    tailrec fun tryGuess(guess: Double): Double {
        val next = f(guess)
        return if (closeEnough(guess, next)) next else tryGuess(next)
    }
    return tryGuess(firstGuess)
}
@end example

@noindent
For example, we can use this method to approximate the fixed point of the
cosine function, starting with 1 as an initial approximation:@footnote{Try
this during a boring lecture: set your calculator to radians and then just
press the cosine button until you obtain the fixed point.}

@example kotlin
fixedPoint(::cos, 1.0)
// => 0.7390822985224024
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, we can find a solution to the equation
@math{{y = \sin y + \cos y}}:

@example kotlin
fixedPoint({ y -> sin(y) + cos(y) }, 1.0)
// => 1.2587315962971173
@end example

@noindent
The fixed-point process is reminiscent of the process we used for finding
square roots in @ref{1.1.7}.  Both are based on the idea of repeatedly
improving a guess until the result satisfies some criterion.  In fact, we can
readily formulate the square-root computation as a fixed-point search.
Computing the square root of some number @math{x} requires finding a @math{y} such
that @math{{y^2 = x}}.  Putting this equation into the equivalent form
@math{{y = x / y}}, we recognize that we are looking for a fixed point of the
function@footnote{@math{\mapsto} (pronounced ``maps to'') is the
mathematician's way of writing @code{lambda}.  @math{{x \mapsto y}} means
``the function whose value at @math{x} is @math{y},'' that is, the function
@code{{ x -> y }}.} @math{{y \mapsto x / y}}, and we can therefore try to
compute square roots as

@example kotlin
fun sqrtNaive(x: Double): Double = fixedPoint({ y -> x / y }, 1.0)
@end example

@noindent
Unfortunately, this fixed-point search does not converge.  Consider an
initial guess @math{{y_1}}.  The next guess is @math{{y_2 = x / y_1}} and the
next guess is @math{{y_3 = x / y_2 = x / (x / y_1) = y_1}}.  This results in
an infinite loop in which the two guesses @math{{y_1}} and @math{{y_2}}
repeat over and over, oscillating about the answer.

One way to control such oscillations is to prevent the guesses from changing
so much.  Since the answer is always between our guess @math{y} and
@math{{x / y}}, we can make a new guess that is not as far from @math{y} as
@math{{x / y}} by averaging @math{y} with @math{{x / y}}, so that the next guess
after @math{y} is @math{{(1/2)(y + x/y)}} instead of @math{{x / y}}.  The
process of making such a sequence of guesses is simply the process of looking
for a fixed point of @math{{y \mapsto (1/2)(y + x/y)}}:

@example kotlin
fun sqrtByAverageDamp(x: Double): Double =
    fixedPoint({ y -> average(y, x / y) }, 1.0)

sqrtByAverageDamp(2.0)
// => 1.4142135623746899
@end example

@noindent
(Note that @math{{y = (1/2)(y + x / y)}} is a simple transformation of the
equation @math{{y = x / y}}: to derive it, add @math{y} to both sides of the
equation and divide by 2.)

With this modification, the square-root function works.  In fact, if we
unravel the definitions, we can see that the sequence of approximations to the
square root generated here is precisely the same as the one generated by our
original square-root function of @ref{1.1.7}.  This approach of averaging
successive approximations to a solution, a technique that we call
@newterm{average damping}, often aids the convergence of fixed-point searches.

@quotation
